# Dependencies

A dependency is new when a manifest gains it, including development and build
dependencies. A PR that adds one includes all of these:

1. **A reason.** Under "What changes and why", one line per new dependency
   giving its name, its licence, and why it's needed, for example
   `h5py (BSD-3-Clause): reads HDF5 checkpoint files, which the standard library can't`.
2. **A committed lockfile.** Add the dependency with the package manager
   (`uv add`, `pnpm add`, `cargo add`), and commit the lockfile it writes in
   the same commit as the manifest. C and C++ have no lockfile, so pin an
   exact version instead: a vcpkg `builtin-baseline`, or a FetchContent
   `GIT_TAG` set to a full commit hash, or a `URL` with `URL_HASH`.
3. **A passing licence check**, where one exists:

   | Language | Licence check |
   |---|---|
   | Rust | `cargo deny check licenses` passes. Task T8 in `docs/template-plan.md` adds cargo-deny; until then, the reviewer checks the licence in the reason line. |
   | Python, TypeScript, C and C++ | None exists. The reviewer checks the licence in the reason line. |

   Never widen the list of allowed licences to make a check pass. If a
   dependency's licence isn't allowed, say so in the PR and let a person
   decide.

Never edit a lockfile by hand. On a lockfile merge conflict, resolve the
manifest first, then check out the base branch's lockfile and rerun the lock
command (`uv lock`, `pnpm install`, or `cargo update --workspace`). This keeps
the base branch's versions and adds only what the merged manifest needs.
Deleting the lockfile and locking from scratch would instead upgrade every
dependency.
